      "headline": "The Hall of Mirrors: Breaking Addiction Myths",
      "keyInsight": "Most addiction advice treats cravings like a mysterious, incurable storm inside your blood. People enter support groups expecting a cure, yet they are told they will always stay sick. That creates a bizarre paradox. If you believe you are helpless, every urge becomes proof of your brokenness. You end up studying your cravings instead of ending them. It is like calling an exterminator who moves into your guest room to feed the termites. The moment you see that recovery is not an endless, fragile job, the funhouse mirrors shatter. You do not need lifelong therapy to put down a glass. You only need to stop falling for the reflection that says you cannot live without it.",
      "content": "Imagine walking through a carnival funhouse full of bent mirrors. In one mirror your head looks huge. In another your legs vanish completely. If you believe those glass reflections, you will panic and call an ambulance. Traditional recovery programs often build a similar maze of glass. They tell you that your urge to drink is a permanent disease. They tell you that you are fundamentally powerless over your own hands.\n\nThink about driving a car down the highway. If a loud passenger in the back seat yells at you to drive off a cliff, do you hand them the steering wheel? Of course not. You keep your hands on the wheel and focus on the road ahead. Traditional recovery often asks you to pull over into a ditch. It suggests having a daily conversation with the rowdy passenger about why they love cliffs.\n\nWhen you spend years analyzing your cravings, you water the weeds in your garden. Weeds do not need deep analysis. They just need to be pulled out by the roots. Once you see that the loud voice in your mind is just a reflex, the spell breaks. You step out of the hall of mirrors and walk back into the sunlight.",
