      "headline": "Hello in There: Spotting the Impostor",
      "keyInsight": "Meet your uninvited house guest. It lives down in the basement of your skull, chewing on old survival circuits. It has zero muscles, zero fingers, and zero access to your bank account. In fact, it cannot lift a single glass or open a bottle on its own. It is completely paralyzed without your voluntary muscles. So how does it win? By becoming a brilliant ventriloquist. It throws its voice into your head until you swear you are the one speaking. It whispers, 'Just one taste won't hurt.' The instant you knock on the basement door and say, 'Hello in there, I hear you,' the ventriloquist panics. Once caught red-handed, its grand commands turn into helpless squeaks.",
      "content": "Picture hiring a pushy sales rep who follows you around the grocery store. Every time you walk past the bakery, they grab your elbow. They plead with you to buy a tray of glazed donuts. You can easily see the salesperson standing next to your cart. Because you see them as separate from yourself, you can laugh and keep walking. You do not buy donuts just to quiet them down.\n\nNow imagine that same pushy rep hiding behind a potted plant with a tiny megaphone. If they throw their voice so it sounds like your own conscience, you might believe you are starving for sugar. Addiction works through this exact psychological ventriloquism. The primitive brain cannot move your feet, so it whispers sweet rationalizations to your thinking mind.\n\nCatching the impostor is an exciting game of mental tag. When a sudden urge strikes, do not panic or sweat. Just knock on the wall and say hello. Recognize that the craving is merely a twitch from an ancient survival reflex. You are the sole pilot of the airplane. The noisy stowaway in the baggage compartment has no flight controls.",
